{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83ed65727ba91c8d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9001\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9001\n11\t\n12\t**Strategy**: Surface model deployment status in the RHOAI Dashboard\n13\t**Triage**: Full decomposition (not below-threshold, not docs-only). The strategy\n14\tspans multiple distinct work streams — Dashboard code implementation (HLR-1\n15\tthrough HLR-4) plus substantive product documentation authoring (HLR-5, new doc\n16\ttopics) — and two affected components owned by different teams, so the\n17\tbelow-threshold escape conditions do not apply.\n18\t\n19\t## Step 1 — Strategy Structure\n20\t\n21\t- **High Level Requirements**: HLR-1 (P0), HLR-2 (P0), HLR-3 (P1), HLR-4 (P2),\n22\t  HLR-5 (P2). Present and complete.\n23\t- **Affected Components**: Dashboard, KServe (plain list — no \"what changes\" or\n24\t  owner-team columns; inferred from architecture context, see Health Warnings).\n25\t- **Impacted Teams**: missing.\n26\t- **Dependencies table**: missing.\n27\t- **Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then)**: missing — epic acceptance criteria\n28\t  were derived from the HLRs.\n29\t- **Non-Functional Requirements**: missing — HLR-3's 30-second freshness bound\n30\t  acts as an implicit NFR.\n31\t- **Staff Engineer Input**: absent — no overrides to log.\n32\t\n33\t## Component Graph\n34\t\n35\t| Component | Classification | Owner team (from architecture context) | Handling |\n36\t|-----------|----------------|----------------------------------------|----------|\n37\t| Dashboard | Active (code changes: status surfacing in model serving views) | RHOAI Dashboard | Implementation epic E001 |\n38\t| KServe | Passive (no code changes; conditions and events already exposed) | RHOAI Serving | Strategy relies on KServe's existing InferenceService status contract. Because the passive component is owned by a different team than the active component, a separate validation Implementation epic (E002) owned by RHOAI Serving confirms the contract; this is not folded into the Dashboard epic's ACs. |\n39\t| Documentation | Docs target for HLR-5 (substantive new content) | RHOAI Documentation (assumed — see Health Warnings) | docs-authoring Implementation epic E003 |\n40\t\n41\tAll three component names are canonical entries in `.context/rhai-components.txt`;\n42\tno non-obvious mappings were needed. Dashboard and KServe both have architecture\n43\tcontext files (rhoai-3.0). Documentation has no architecture context file but is\n44\ta canonical component handled through the Red Hat docs content process — no\n45\tonboarding chain is generated for it (see Health Warnings).\n46\t\n47\t## Investigation Epics\n48\t\n49\tNone. The strategy's only open-questions entry states \"None blocking — KServe\n50\talready exposes the needed conditions on the InferenceService status\", and no\n51\tconditional ADRs, pending reviews, or assumptions that would change epic\n52\texistence/scope appear anywhere in the strategy. The remaining unknowns\n53\t(e.g., polling vs. watch for the 30-second freshness, exact condition-to-status\n54\tmapping) are implementation details for the Dashboard team, captured as\n55\tacceptance criteria, not genuine unknowns.\n56\t\n57\t## Epic List\n58\t\n59\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n60\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n61\t| RHAISTRAT-9001-E001 | Surface InferenceService deployment status in Dashboard model serving views | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P0 |\n62\t| RHAISTRAT-9001-E002 | Validate KServe InferenceService status-condition contract for Dashboard consumption | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | KServe | P1 |\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-9001-E003 | Document deployment status values and Failed-deployment troubleshooting | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | Documentation | P1 |\n64\t\n65\t## Dependency DAG\n66\t\n67\t```mermaid\n68\tgraph TD\n69\t    E001[\"E001 Dashboard status surfacing (P0)\"] --> E003[\"E003 Status documentation (P1)\"]\n70\t    E002[\"E002 KServe condition contract validation (P1)\"] --> E003\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\t## DAG Justification\n74\t\n75\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n76\t|------|------|-----------|\n77\t| (root) E001 | — | Dashboard implementation is unblocked: the InferenceService status conditions and events it consumes already exist in-platform (architecture context, kserve.md); the strategy marks nothing blocking. |\n78\t| (root) E002 | Rule 14 | Validation of an existing contract in a different repo (kserve) with no artifact produced that E001 builds against — the conditions already exist; E002 confirms them. Parallel by design; a serial edge would mis-sequence independent work. |\n79\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by all Implementation epics — docs describe what was built. |\n80\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | Same: documentation of status values must reflect the validated condition semantics, not just the Dashboard rendering. |\n81\t\n82\tNo `konflux-onboarding` edges apply: Dashboard and KServe are both already\n83\tKonflux-onboarded per architecture context, and Documentation is handled through\n84\tthe docs content process. No conditional branches (no Investigation epics).\n85\tdocs-authoring edges do not trigger priority inheritance (Step 4 exception);\n86\tE003's P1 comes from the strategy frontmatter priority `Major` → P1.\n87\t\n88\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n89\t\n90\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n91\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n92\t| HLR-1 (status shown per deployed model) | P0 | E001 | |\n93\t| HLR-2 (status detail view: conditions + events) | P0 | E001 | Same logical change and same status-condition data as HLR-1; shares the model serving views surface. |\n94\t| HLR-3 (≤30s freshness, no manual reload) | P1 | E001 | Coupled to the same views; mechanism (polling vs. watch) is an implementation detail. |\n95\t| HLR-4 (filter/sort list by status) | P2 | E001 | Bundled, not split: shares the deployed-models list UI surface and status data with the P0 work — not \"distinct and deferrable\" under Step 4. |\n96\t| HLR-5 (product documentation) | P2 | E003 | Substantive content authoring — distinct work stream, separate docs-authoring epic. |\n97\t\n98\tAll five HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. Priorities: E001 inherits\n99\tP0 (highest of HLR-1..4). E003 is P1 via the docs-authoring priority exception\n100\t(strategy priority Major → P1), exempt from inheritance.\n101\t\n102\t## Health Warnings\n103\t\n104\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n105\t   Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria,\n106\t   and no NFR section; the Affected Components list lacks change/owner detail.\n107\t   Team assignments were inferred from architecture context (RHOAI Dashboard,\n108\t   RHOAI Serving) and the Documentation owner was assumed (see below). Epic\n109\t   acceptance criteria were derived from HLRs in lieu of strategy ACs.\n110\t2. **Documentation owner assumed**: the Documentation component has no\n111\t   architecture context file, so its owning team could not be verified;\n112\t   \"RHOAI Documentation\" is a placeholder assumption to confirm with the\n113\t   strategy author.\n114\t3. **HLR-4 bundling**: the P2 filter/sort requirement is bundled into the P0\n115\t   epic because it shares the deployed-models list UI surface and status data.\n116\t   If the team prefers to defer it, it can be split later — flagged for human\n117\t   verification, not a priority inversion.\n118\t4. **E002 priority judgment**: the KServe contract validation is set P1 because\n119\t   the strategy explicitly marks it non-blocking and it runs in parallel with\n120\t   the P0 Dashboard work; however, it validates the data source behind the P0\n121\t   HLRs, so a reviewer may reasonably argue for P0. Flagged for verification.\n122\t5. **Implicit NFR**: HLR-3's 30-second freshness bound is a performance\n123\t   requirement with no NFR section to reconcile against; the freshness\n124\t   mechanism is left as an implementation decision.\n125\t\n126\t## Tiered Delivery\n127\t\n128\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing. All work is\n129\tin-platform.\n130\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-831fc1fe7ac21f51","agent_id":"ad64f453705a37765","session_id":"3b82bbf4-97d2-4391-a50a-11f44f5d64aa","uuid":"75e0d9f9-5cae-4e31-a627-e377808b2d4e","timestamp":"2026-10-21T23:18:37.372Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-83ed65727ba91c8d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9001 decomposition"}
