board work (and shrinks further once HLR-4 is split out per Criterion\n108\t1).\n109\t\n110\tMinor (does not reduce score): the condition-to-display-state mapping is\n111\ttouched by both E002 (documents the recommended mapping) and E001 (derives\n112\tthe mapping it renders, with a \"coordinate\" note). This is consumable\n113\thandoff, not duplicate construction, so it is not scope duplication — but\n114\tthe revise pass could sharpen which epic owns the authoritative mapping.\n115\t\n116\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n117\t\n118\tNo Investigation epics exist, which is correct: the strategy's only open\n119\tquestion is explicitly \"None blocking,\" and the remaining unknowns\n120\t(refresh mechanism, condition mapping) are implementation decisions, not\n121\tepic-existence-changing unknowns. All three epics are Implementation and\n122\teach produces a concrete artifact (Dashboard code, a documented\n123\tcontract-validation/mapping deliverable, published docs). All `gated_by`\n124\tand `gate_failure_impact` fields are null, consistent with no gates.\n125\t\n126\tMinor (does not reduce score): E002 is validation-only and produces a\n127\treport rather than code, which is Investigation-shaped at the margins. It\n128\tis defensible as Implementation here because the strategy asserts the\n129\tcontract already exists and is non-blocking (nothing downstream changes\n130\texistence/scope based on its outcome), and it hands off a concrete\n131\tartifact. If the revise pass reconsiders it, the alternative would be\n132\tacceptance criteria on E001 rather than a gate.\n133\t\n134\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n135\t\n136\tAll three epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals`\n137\t(none carry `investigation_signals`); all three ai-signals files exist with\n138\trationales that match the frontmatter values. Spot-checks against context:\n139\t\n140\t- E001 `pattern_precedent: 1` is corroborated — the Dashboard architecture\n141\t  context documents existing status views and distributed workload status\n142\t  surfacing in the same console.\n143\t- E001/E002 `architecture_claims: 1` is corroborated — the KServe\n144\t  architecture context confirms serving.kserve.io/v1beta1 exposes Ready and\n145\t  PredictorReady conditions plus Kubernetes events, in both serverless and\n146\t  raw deployment modes.\n147\t- E002 `change_specificity: 1` is reasonable — the checks are enumerable\n148\t  (named conditions, both deployment modes, event observability, defined\n149\t  deliverable).\n150\t- E003's cautious values (`repo_access: 0`, `existing_foundation: 0`,\n151\t  `human_process_gates: -1`) correctly reflect the absent Documentation\n152\t  architecture context file and the mandatory docs technical review.\n153\t\n154\tNo signal value contradicts the rubric or the strategy content.\n155\t\n156\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n157\t\n158\tEvery epic has testable, HLR-derived Given/When/Then or verifiable ACs.\n159\tRule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E003 (docs-authoring) has\n160\tthe \"technically reviewed against the implemented behavior\" AC; E001\n161\tadditionally carries a build-pipeline-green AC. No replacement/rollback AC\n162\tis required (no component is being replaced). Out-of-scope boundaries\n163\t(alerting, status history, ModelMesh) are correctly restated as negative\n164\tACs. No epic body references siblings by draft ID — cross-epic references\n165\tuse team names (\"the Dashboard effort\", \"the serving team's validation\").\n166\t\n167\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n168\t\n169\tCombined epic scope covers the full strategy: HLR-1..4 in E001 (with HLR-4\n170\tmis-bundled per Criterion 1, but covered), HLR-5 in E003, with E002\n171\tcovering the cross-team KServe contract validation. Out-of-scope items are\n172\trespected in all epics. The docs-authoring obligation implied by HLR-5 is\n173\tmet by E003. Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: the\n174\t\"none