{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b64fbbd866a1884b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9001-E001\n3\ttitle: Surface InferenceService deployment status in Dashboard model serving views\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9001\n5\tcomponent: Dashboard\n6\tteam: RHOAI Dashboard\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tai_signals:\n10\t  change_specificity: 0\n11\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n12\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n13\t  existing_foundation: 1\n14\t  open_questions: 1\n15\t  external_dependency: 1\n16\t  human_process_gates: 0\n17\t  repo_access: 1\n18\t  architecture_claims: 1\n19\timplementation_type: null\n20\tdependencies: []\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 6\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Surface InferenceService deployment status in Dashboard model serving views\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic delivers deployment status visibility for KServe InferenceServices\n34\tinside the RHOAI Dashboard. Data scientists will see at a glance whether each\n35\tdeployed model is Ready, Progressing, or Failed, can drill into the underlying\n36\tcondition messages and recent events for failed or progressing deployments,\n37\tand see status stay current without a manual page reload. This removes the\n38\tneed to leave the console and run `oc get inferenceservice` or read Kubernetes\n39\tevents directly.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Add a deployment status (Ready, Progressing, Failed) for each deployed model\n44\t  in the Dashboard model serving views, derived from the InferenceService CR\n45\t  status conditions (e.g. Ready, PredictorReady) and related Kubernetes events.\n46\t- Add a status detail view for a deployment showing the underlying condition\n47\t  messages and recent events, reachable for failed or progressing deployments.\n48\t- Keep the displayed status within 30 seconds of the actual InferenceService\n49\t  state without requiring a manual page reload (refresh mechanism — e.g.\n50\t  polling or watch — is an implementation decision for the Dashboard team).\n51\t- Filter and sort by status (HLR-4) is not part of this epic: it is a\n52\t  distinct, deferrable capability delivered by a separate P2 epic owned by the\n53\t  same team, so it can be planned and deferred independently of this P0 work.\n54\t- KServe itself requires no code changes: the conditions and events are\n55\t  already exposed on the InferenceService status. The serving team's\n56\t  validation of that status-condition contract runs in parallel with this\n57\t  work and owns the authoritative recommended condition-to-display-state\n58\t  mapping; this epic consumes that recommendation as a non-blocking input and\n59\t  coordinates any rendering deviations with the serving team.\n60\t- Out of scope per strategy: alerting/notifications on status transitions,\n61\t  status history / timeline views, and ModelMesh deployments (KServe\n62\t  InferenceService only).\n63\t\n64\t## Acceptance Criteria\n65\t\n66\t- Given a deployed KServe InferenceService, when a user opens the Dashboard\n67\t  model serving views, then each deployed model shows a status of Ready,\n68\t  Progressing, or Failed (HLR-1).\n69\t- Given a failed or progressing deployment, when the user opens its status\n70\t  detail view, then the underlying condition messages and recent events for\n71\t  that deployment are shown (HLR-2).\n72\t- Given a change in the actual InferenceService state, the displayed status\n73\t  reflects it within 30 seconds, without a manual page reload (HLR-3).\n74\t- The Ready/Progressing/Failed mapping is derived from the InferenceService\n75\t  status conditions as documented in the KServe architecture context\n76\t  (serving.kserve.io/v1beta1 conditions such as Ready and PredictorReady).\n77\t- ModelMesh deployments are not covered by the status display (strategy\n78\t  out-of-scope).\n79\t- The Dashboard's existing build pipeline remains green with these changes.\n80\t\n81\t## HLR Traceability\n82\t\n83\t- HLR-1 (P0): status shown per deployed model — this epic.\n84\t- HLR-2 (P0): status detail view with conditions and events — this epic.\n85\t- HLR-3 (P1): 30-second freshness without manual reload — this epic.\n86\t- HLR-4 (P2): filter and sort by status — separate P2 epic owned by the same\n87\t  team (distinct, deferrable capability; split so it can be planned and\n88\t  deferred independently of this P0 work).\n89\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9502baf080c85957","agent_id":"a1a3ac3d2f4f3ae01","session_id":"3b82bbf4-97d2-4391-a50a-11f44f5d64aa","uuid":"cba0f8a5-ece1-45cc-a7ff-5442db79c388","timestamp":"2026-10-21T23:29:37.408Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b64fbbd866a1884b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9001 decomposition"}
